    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY A REFUTATION IS THE VALUABLE RESULT HERE. Every reduction kernel, every tensor-parallel all-reduce and every reordering optimizer relies on being allowed to re-bracket a sum. This fact says precisely how often that is wrong at fp8 E5M2: 427036 of 16777216 triples under RNE, and 5375600 -- nearly a third -- under round-toward-zero. That is a measured density with a named witness, not an aphorism.\n\nTHE FINDING ABOUT THE ORACLES. This claim could not be cross-checked against either industrial FP solver, for two DIFFERENT reasons, and both were measured on 2026-08-14 rather than assumed:\n  * z3 4.13.3 returns `unknown` with `(:reason-unknown \"addition/subtract with ebits > sbits not supported\")`. E5M2 has 5 exponent bits and 3 significand bits, so every fp8 addition query is outside its fpa2bv tactic.\n  * bitwuzla 0.9.1 errors out: \"Unsupported experimental floating-point format (non-experimental: Float16, Float32, Float64, Float128)\". The `--fpexp` escape named in that message is a BUILD option, not a runtime flag -- passing it to the binary is rejected as an invalid option, so this build cannot be talked into it.\nSo at exactly the width where a claim can be settled by brute force, the two solvers a practitioner would reach for decline. axeyum decides it, and the LLVM-APFloat enumeration is what independently confirms the answer. Note the complementary gap in the other direction: axeyum REFUSES fp8 E4M3, correctly, because E4M3 has no infinities and an all-ones NaN and is therefore not an IEEE format -- `arithmetic_format_supported` gates it out, while z3 accepts (_ FloatingPoint 4 4) happily.\n\nROUTE. `search-certificate`: the settlement is a replayable witness, matching the precedent of F:affirming-the-consequent. Refuting a universally quantified claim costs one triple; establishing one costs the whole space -- and here we paid both, because the enumeration also gives the density.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
